<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Corazon Aquino</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1933">1933</a>–<a href="/years/2009">2009</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Cory Aquino</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Corazon Aquino was president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> from 1986 to 1992, the first woman to hold the office. The widow of the opposition leader Benigno Aquino Jr., murdered in 1983, she challenged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-marcos">Ferdinand Marcos</a> in the 1986 snap election, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/people-power-revolution">People Power Revolution</a> drove Marcos into exile. Her government introduced the 1987 constitution and survived several coup attempts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/corazon-aquino.jpg" alt="Corazon Aquino" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Airman Gerald B. Johnson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Corazon_Aquino_1986.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>María Corazón Sumulong Cojuangco was born on 25 January 1933 in Paniqui, Tarlac, the sixth of eight children of the businessman and former congressman José Cojuangco and Demetria Sumulong, a pharmacist. Both parents came from prominent political families. While studying law in 1953 she met Benigno Aquino Jr., known as Ninoy, and she gave up her studies to marry him on 11 October 1954. They had five children, among them Benigno Aquino III. Her husband became a leading critic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-marcos">Ferdinand Marcos</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After her husband was assassinated on 21 August 1983 she became a figurehead of the opposition. Marcos announced a snap election on 3 November 1985, held on 7 February 1986. A petition with 1.2 million signatures urged her to run, and she declared her candidacy on 3 December, with Salvador Laurel as her running mate. The Batasang Pambansa proclaimed Marcos the winner, which prompted allegations of fraud and her call for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-disobedience">civil disobedience</a>.</p><p>On 22 February 1986 Juan Ponce Enrile and General Fidel V. Ramos announced their defection from the Marcos government. Cardinal Sin appealed over the Church's Radyo Veritas, and millions gathered on Epifanio de los Santos Avenue. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/people-power-revolution">People Power Revolution</a> ousted Marcos, and Aquino, who had held no earlier political office, was sworn in on 25 February 1986.</p><p>She issued a provisional Freedom Constitution, and the 1987 Constitution limited the presidency to a single six-year term. Her government produced the Family Code of 1987 and the 1991 Local Government Code, and in 1989 created the Autonomous Region in Muslim Mindanao after talks with the Moro National Liberation Front. It faced coup attempts, the deadliest in August 1987 leaving 53 dead and the most serious in December 1989. Marines killed 12 farmers at the Mendiola Massacre in January 1987. Aquino declined to seek re-election and left office in 1992.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>She chaired the Benigno S. Aquino Jr. Foundation and spoke abroad on democracy and human rights. She died of colorectal cancer on 1 August 2009, and her family declined a state funeral. Time named her Woman of the Year in 1986, and she received the Ramon Magsaysay Award in 1998.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Corazon Aquino” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After days of peaceful mass protest, Ferdinand Marcos fled Manila for Hawaii and <b>Corazon Aquino</b> was sworn in as president.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-25">25 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Ferdinand Marcos imposed martial law from 1972, ruling through cronies until the People Power Revolution of 1986 brought <b>Corazon Aquino</b> to power.</blockquote><a href="/state/philippines">Philippines</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Paniqui, in what is now <a href="/countries/philippines">the Philippines</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=15.67,120.58&amp;year=1982" aria-label="Paniqui on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7840 1581 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Corazon Aquino"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8290 1851h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8290 1851h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=15.67,120.58&amp;year=1982">What was here in 1982? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
